        "faq_0": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is Tesamorelin?",
            "answer": "<p>Tesamorelin is a stabilized synthetic analogue of growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) studied for its effects on pituitary signalling and the endogenous GH/IGF-1 axis. ELIX LABS supplies it exclusively for laboratory research.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is GHRH?",
            "answer": "<p>GHRH is a hypothalamic signalling peptide that acts at the pituitary gland to regulate pulsatile secretion of endogenous growth hormone.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Tesamorelin interact with the GHRH receptor?",
            "answer": "<p>Tesamorelin is studied as a ligand of the pituitary GHRH receptor. Receptor engagement activates GHRH-linked intracellular pathways associated with endogenous growth-hormone release.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Tesamorelin influence GH and IGF-1 signalling?",
            "answer": "<p>Research examines whether pituitary GHRH-receptor activation increases pulsatile endogenous GH secretion, which can influence downstream IGF-1 production. Responses depend on the model and experimental or clinical context.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is Tesamorelin the same as growth hormone?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Tesamorelin is a GHRH analogue investigated for stimulation of endogenous pituitary GH release; it is not directly administered growth hormone.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is Tesamorelin studied in relation to visceral adipose tissue?",
            "answer": "<p>The GH/IGF-1 axis can influence lipid metabolism, lipolysis and body composition. Studies therefore measure visceral adipose tissue in defined populations and controlled settings, without establishing a universal effect.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_6": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does research on visceral fat automatically indicate overall weight loss?",
            "answer": "<p>No. A change in one fat compartment does not necessarily produce or predict a comparable change in total body weight. Each outcome must be interpreted independently within the study design.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_7": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Tesamorelin differ from Retatrutide?",
            "answer": "<p>Tesamorelin primarily activates the GHRH receptor and modulates endogenous GH/IGF-1 signalling. Retatrutide is investigated as an agonist of GLP-1, GIP and glucagon receptors, so their mechanisms and evidence bases are distinct.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_8": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Tesamorelin differ from Cagrilintide?",
            "answer": "<p>Tesamorelin is a GHRH analogue, whereas Cagrilintide is an amylin analogue studied at amylin-receptor complexes. Tesamorelin is not an amylin analogue, and neither comparison implies equivalent outcomes.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_9": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How should lyophilized Tesamorelin be stored for research?",
            "answer": "<p>Keep sealed lyophilized material refrigerated at 2–8°C, dry and protected from light. Follow the batch documentation and the laboratory’s validated handling and stability procedures.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_10": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is the ELIX LABS product intended for human use?",
            "answer": "<p>No. This ELIX LABS product is exclusively for laboratory research and is not for human or veterinary use, diagnosis, treatment or consumption. It must not be treated as equivalent to an authorized pharmaceutical product.</p>"
          }
        },
